<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Hidetada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1579">1579</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Hidetada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Hidetada was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. He succeeded his father as shogun in 1605 while Ieyasu continued to exercise power in retirement. His rule helped consolidate the new regime, and accounts of the Tokugawa Shogunate discuss him in the context of the dynasty's foundation and the orderly transfer of power within the Tokugawa house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Hidetada" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The author creates a file. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hidetada2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hidetada was born on 2 May 1579, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> by Lady Saigō. In 1590 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> took the eleven-year-old Hidetada as a hostage to keep Ieyasu from defecting to the Hōjō in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>, and in 1592 presided over his coming-of-age ceremony, when he took the name Hidetada. As the eldest surviving son and Ieyasu's favourite, he was named heir, and he returned to his father in 1593. In 1595 he married Oeyo, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> and adopted daughter of Hideyoshi, after the death of his first wife O-Hime in 1591. They had two sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a> and Tokugawa Tadanaga, and several daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hidetada led 16,000 of his father's men against the Uesugi in Shinano in 1600 and was ordered to march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, but the Sanada clan tied his force down and he arrived too late for his father's victory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> was furious and was dissuaded by advisers from punishing him. Emperor Go-Yōzei made Ieyasu shōgun in 1603, and in 1605 Ieyasu abdicated in favour of Hidetada to set a dynastic pattern, though he kept significant power until his death in 1616.</p><p>In 1612 Hidetada arranged the marriage of his daughter Sen to Toyotomi Hideyori, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s dismay, and when this failed to end Hideyori's intrigues father and son brought an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a> in 1614–1615 they disagreed on tactics. Hideyori and his mother killed themselves, and Hideyori's infant son was killed; only Sen was spared.</p><p>After 1616 Hidetada took control of the bakufu. He improved relations with the imperial court by marrying his daughter Kazuko to Emperor Go-Mizunoo, and their daughter eventually became Empress Meishō. Edo was heavily developed in his time. In 1623 he resigned in favour of Iemitsu and, as Ōgosho, kept effective power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As Ōgosho Hidetada enacted anti-Christian measures that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> had only considered. He banned Christian books, forced Christian daimyō to commit suicide, ordered other Christians to apostatise on pain of death, and in 1628 had fifty-five Christians, Japanese and foreign, burned with their children at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> for refusing to renounce their faith. He died on 14 March 1632, and was buried in the Taitoku-in Mausoleum in Edo. The historian Michifumi Isoda held that the policy of total isolation under Hidetada weakened the military of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> in the long run.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokugawa Hidetada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He secured the shogunal title in 1603 and passed it to his son <b>Hidetada</b> in 1605 while retaining real power as retired shogun, which established the hereditary principle that kept the office in Tokugawa hands through fifteen shoguns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was part of the persecution of Christians by the shogun <b>Tokugawa Hidetada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.71,137.73&amp;year=1613" aria-label="Hamamatsu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tokugawa Hidetada"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4144 4552h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4144 4552h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.71,137.73&amp;year=1613">What was here in 1613? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
